# Coding Conventions — Node.js / Express

## Error Handling

Always use `next(err)` to pass errors to the global error handler:

```javascript
// ✅ Correct — in async controller
async function getUser(req, res, next) {
  try {
    const user = await userService.findById(req.params.id);
    if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found' });
    res.json(user);
  } catch (err) {
    next(err); // pass to global error handler
  }
}

// ❌ Wrong — handling errors inside each controller individually
async function getUser(req, res) {
  const user = await userService.findById(req.params.id);
  // if this throws, Express won't catch it
}
```

---

## Async Route Handler

Wrap async handlers so Express can catch unhandled rejections:

```javascript
// Helper wrapper (place in utils/asyncHandler.js)
const asyncHandler = (fn) => (req, res, next) =>
  Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);

// Use in router
router.get('/users/:id', asyncHandler(userController.getOne));
```

---

## Response Format

Standardize response format throughout the codebase:

```javascript
// Success
res.status(200).json({ data: result });

// Error (in global error handler)
res.status(err.status || 500).json({ error: err.message || 'Internal Server Error' });
```

---

## Naming Convention

| Type | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| File | camelCase | `userService.js`, `authController.js` |
| Function / Variable | camelCase | `findById`, `userId` |
| Constant | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | `MAX_RETRY`, `JWT_EXPIRY` |
| Route | kebab-case | `/api/user-profiles` |
| Env var | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | `DATABASE_URL`, `JWT_SECRET` |
